# ── Derived-only (EDB/IDB) pins ───────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Run by `just verify-pins` (nibli-pin, native).
#
# WHAT THIS GUARDS. In nibli KR a rule ADDS a derivation path; it never REMOVES
# assertability. So a knowledge base that DERIVES a credential still lets anyone
# assert one directly, and an article whose stated guarantee is "credentials
# derive from selection, never from assertion" is simply false.
#
#     derived_only("permits").
#
# closes the relation: thereafter only a rule may conclude it, and a direct
# ground assertion is refused fail-closed at assert time with the whole
# assertion unwound atomically. The attack becomes "edit the knowledge base"
# instead of "write one fact" — loud, diffable, reviewable. That change in the
# SHAPE of the attack is the point, and it is why this rejects rather than lints.
#
# The three refusals below are the three exploits an adversarial audit found,
# each reduced to its minimal shape. They are pinned as REFUSALS with an error
# class plus a message needle, so a misspelled predicate (a Syntax error) can
# never satisfy them.

# ── the declarations ──
derived_only("permits").
derived_only("authority").
derived_only("fit").

# ── the derivation routes these protect (must all still work) ──
all $a: choose(Electorate, $a) & ~rotten($a) & ~broken($a) -> permits(Review, $a).
choose(Electorate, Gia).

person(Ara).
person(Sock).

# ── EXPLOIT A: seize an authority credential with one asserted fact ──
# The whistleblower shield gates on the exposed party being an `authority`,
# which is supposed to derive only from selection. One fact made an assailant
# unjailable.
:refuse reasoning /`authority` is declared derived-only/
authority(Pax).

# ── EXPLOIT B: seat sock puppets to drive the multi-sig ──
# The audit rule requires two INDEPENDENT Review-credentialed auditors. Neither
# puppet was ever seated by the Electorate, yet every guard on the rule passed
# because the credential could simply be asserted.
:refuse reasoning /`permits` is declared derived-only/
permits(Review, Sock).

# The converted-alias spelling must not be a bypass. `permitted` is the x1<->x2
# conversion of `permits` and compiles to the SAME IR relation, so it is closed
# by the same declaration — otherwise the whole guarantee is one synonym away
# from worthless.
:refuse reasoning /`permits` is declared derived-only/
permitted(Sock).

# ── EXPLOIT C: silence a breach marker by asserting eligibility ──
# Home-confinement eligibility is derived; asserting it directly suppressed the
# unearned-placement flag.
:refuse reasoning /`fit` is declared derived-only/
fit(Ruk, Homestay).

# ── ATOMICITY: a mixed conjunction must land NEITHER conjunct ──
# Otherwise the legal half of a smuggling assertion still lands.
:refuse reasoning /`permits` is declared derived-only/
person(Puppet) & permits(Review, Puppet).

? person(Puppet).
# => FALSE

# ── THE OTHER HALF: closing must not break derivation ──
# A refusal-only suite is satisfied by an engine that refuses everything.
? permits(Review, Gia).
# => TRUE

# An unseated auditor is still simply FALSE — closing the relation did not turn
# the credential into something unknowable.
? permits(Review, Sock).
# => FALSE

# ── SCOPE: unrelated relations stay assertable ──
:accept
person(Bet).

:accept
choose(Electorate, Bet).

# The declaration is queryable — the closure list is inspectable, not hidden
# engine state.
? derived_only("permits").
# => TRUE

:expect-pins 11
